title crypto/eip155_tx.rye — EIP-155 legacy transaction digest in pure Rye, parity-GREEN
prompt take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the crypto/Rye-first road; Rye prioritized, Glow on green Rye; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates
think Read the decision-wave design, the 1024-round itinerary, and REMEMBER. The RLP commit (HEAD) named the next rung outright — "the EIP-155 legacy transaction digest over the GREEN keccak256 and secp256k1." That is the Lindy-first crux-first move: the digest every Ethereum send signs, read for years, self-contained, parity-checkable, authoring no new cryptography.
think Chose crypto/ (not encoding/) as home — this is a cryptographic composition beside eip712, eth_personal_sign, eth_address, hashing and recovering. RLP went in encoding/ to keep the count guard at 41; a genuine crypto composition earns the count guard bump to 42.
think Discovered the elegance: the RLP module's own transaction-shape test already carried the EIP-155 example's exact r/s/v scalars (v=37, r 28ef…6276, s 67cb…6d83). So eip155_tx binds those to the world — the same bytes now recover a real published sender.
think Authored signing_sighash (keccak256 of rlp([nonce,gasPrice,gas,to,value,data,chainId,0,0]) via an internal max_tx_bytes buffer, no allocation), v_parts (the v↔(recovery_id, chainId) split with 27/28 pre-155 and >=35 EIP-155 branches), and recover_sender (ecr.recover then eth.address_from_pubkey), every function carrying contract asserts. Imports RLP across directories through a flat-named symlink shim crypto/encoding_rlp.rye -> ../encoding/rlp.rye, the same pattern bitcoin_address uses for the encoders.
think Parity three ways anyone can replay: (1) the EIP-155 spec's own canonical example hashes byte-for-byte to the spec's stated sighash 0xdaf5a779…4c8e53; (2) the identical RLP preimage hashed by Zig's own std.crypto Keccak-256 gives the same digest, a second independent path; (3) the spec's published (v=37, r, s) recovers through our GREEN ecrecover to exactly the example's published sender 0x9d8a62f6…855a4f, the address of the spec's test key 0x4646…46 — a one-bit encoding error anywhere would break it. Plus a signed-tx round-trip through the RLP decoder and three edge refusals (BadFieldCount, BadV, BadScalarLength).
obs GREEN on the first metal build — the published sighash, the independent Keccak, and the sender recovery all matched at once. TAME style bans clean; the new file is not in the zero-assert ratchet list.
obs Bumped the count guard to 42: registered crypto_eip155_tx_witness in the suite, updated both suite prose lines (seven→eight compositions, forty-one→forty-two), and synced the front doors — crypto/README.md, docs/CRYPTO.md, crypto/CONSTANT_TIME.md. The count-guard bijection witness prints 42, and the full suite greens all 42 files fresh in dependency order.
obs Signing itself stays the custody gate — this module builds the public preimage and recovers from a published signature, never holding a key. A real signature with the maintainer's identity remains gate #3/#4.
loom witness=crypto_eip155_tx_witness green=1 files=42 sighash_bytes=32 tx_fields=9 parity_ways=3
